# Runbook: roll back or roll forward <service>

Owner: <team>. Last exercised: <date, environment>. Review after every use.

## When to use this runbook

Use it when a deployment is the likely cause of user impact: an SLO burn-rate
page fires within the bake window of a release, the canary gate fails, smoke
checks fail after promotion, or support reports a regression tied to the
release.

## 1. Decide in five minutes

| Question | If yes |
| --- | --- |
| Did impact start with or after the latest deploy? | Prefer rollback |
| Is the previous revision, slot content, or digest still available and healthy? | Roll back by traffic switch |
| Did the release run a contract (destructive) migration or a one-way data change? | Do not roll back the app alone; roll forward with a fix or restore data per the migration plan |
| Is the fix a one-line, low-risk change that can pass the pipeline faster than recovery by rollback? | Roll forward through the normal pipeline |
| Is the cause a configuration or flag change? | Revert the configuration or turn the flag off first |

Write the decision and time in the incident channel before acting.

## 2. Roll back

- Container Apps: run the `rollback` workflow with the environment and an
  empty revision (uses the `previous` label), or a named revision, or an older
  attested digest when the revision was purged.
- App Service slots: swap the staging slot back into production; the staging
  slot holds the previous production content after a swap.
- Kubernetes progressive delivery: abort the rollout (for example
  `kubectl argo rollouts abort <name>`), then revert the desired state in Git
  so the controller does not redeploy the failed version.
- Feature flag: turn the flag off for everyone; record the flag change.

Never rebuild an old commit to roll back; redeploy the digest that already ran.

## 3. Verify recovery

- Production smoke check passes.
- Error ratio and latency return to baseline on the dashboard.
- The burn-rate alert resolves; record `restored_at` for delivery metrics.

## 4. Hand off

- Incident record: timeline, decision, commands run, revision or digest
  before and after, and who approved.
- Mark the deployment as a change failure in the deployment export
  (`caused_incident: true`, `restored_at`).
- Block re-promotion of the failed digest until a fix is merged.
- Schedule the post-incident review; add a regression test or a new gate.

